1
00:00:05,659 --> 00:00:08,029
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:08,030 --> 00:00:13,550
This time, let's learn about how to find detail in a picture and finishing.

3
00:00:15,075 --> 00:00:19,550
In a picture, detail refers to small design elements.

4
00:00:19,551 --> 00:00:27,870
For example, high-density patterns, or density expressed by using various materials and colors.

5
00:00:27,950 --> 00:00:35,310
Also, beyond design, elements for the overall completion of a picture can be called detail.

6
00:00:35,311 --> 00:00:43,070
For example, the design of lighting or the flow of the gaze, the placement of contrast, facial expression, hand gesture, pose.

7
00:00:43,770 --> 00:00:48,810
A tidy outline, an appropriate crop, and so on.

8
00:00:48,811 --> 00:00:59,011
This can be expressed as high density, this can be expressed as a well-done design, and this can be expressed as a good finish.

9
00:01:01,125 --> 00:01:09,280
Among these, this one is early-stage work, so I'll set it aside and look at the other two.

10
00:01:10,490 --> 00:01:14,940
So far, I've emphasized drawing big and drawing finely.

11
00:01:14,941 --> 00:01:20,540
I taught that you should render by big masses and then do detail work.

12
00:01:20,541 --> 00:01:24,181
This detail rendering can raise the density of the picture.

13
00:01:27,364 --> 00:01:30,400
First, let's learn about the density of a picture.

14
00:01:30,401 --> 00:01:34,800
Why do I think density is important?

15
00:01:34,801 --> 00:01:46,000
If the overall look or mood of a picture draws the consumer's gaze, then high-density rendering can keep the gaze in the picture a bit longer.

16
00:01:46,001 --> 00:01:49,120
However, raising density should also be done well.

17
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:50,480
How can you do it?

18
00:01:51,919 --> 00:01:55,920
When designing, we learned to concentrate and release.

19
00:01:55,921 --> 00:01:59,840
The parts where you need to give density aren't much different.

20
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:03,840
That's why design and density are always connected.

21
00:02:03,841 --> 00:02:07,600
Density makes the gaze concentrate.

22
00:02:07,601 --> 00:02:14,240
If the picture is high-density overall, there'd be too many places for the eyes to rest here and there.

23
00:02:14,241 --> 00:02:18,041
You'd get tired easily and want to scroll down quickly.

24
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:20,490
Then the answer is out.

25
00:02:21,054 --> 00:02:27,090
In character key art, you can think of giving density to the parts where you concentrate the design.

26
00:02:28,804 --> 00:02:32,681
First, let me look at the parts in my pictures where I attended to density.

27
00:02:33,614 --> 00:02:38,000
In these pictures, I gave density to the helmet here.

28
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:42,780
The gaze can concentrate toward the face.

29
00:02:42,781 --> 00:02:51,820
These aren't designs I created, but the reason I wanted to use these designs in my picture is this density that concentrates on the face.

30
00:02:53,404 --> 00:03:00,680
The time taken to draw this detail part exceeds thirty percent of the whole process.

31
00:03:00,681 --> 00:03:03,720
I wouldn't waste time for no reason.

32
00:03:03,721 --> 00:03:12,760
If you imagine this detail gone, the picture would lose its power.

33
00:03:12,761 --> 00:03:16,120
Since it's a concept art class, let's look at it with concept art.

34
00:03:16,121 --> 00:03:22,401
This character is a game character for which we agreed to concentrate the detail and silhouette on the lower body.

35
00:03:22,839 --> 00:03:27,770
This character especially concentrates density toward the pelvis here.

36
00:03:27,771 --> 00:03:32,330
You can see the rest has just enough detail to naturally support it.

37
00:03:34,459 --> 00:03:40,590
This character has detail extremely concentrated on the neck and shoulders.

38
00:03:40,591 --> 00:03:46,510
In fact, this character was designed this way because only the upper body would appear in the game.

39
00:03:46,511 --> 00:03:50,270
In this way, depending on the purpose, the parts where you need to tighten density can differ.

40
00:03:52,089 --> 00:04:00,540
Here, this character is the same too. It uses yellow metal only on the neck and shoulder area to give a point and density.

41
00:04:03,204 --> 00:04:07,200
As you can see, this character also concentrates all the upper-body detail.

42
00:04:11,055 --> 00:04:18,839
Density should be established by setting the scope of concentrate and release like this, then working on it.

43
00:04:20,695 --> 00:04:24,880
You can concentrate density this way, in various ways.

44
00:04:24,881 --> 00:04:30,880
It can be done by size, by density, or by color difference.

45
00:04:32,035 --> 00:04:42,750
How much to concentrate and how much to release varies by situation, but the one thing you must keep is that you shouldn't give density evenly across the whole thing.

46
00:04:42,751 --> 00:04:44,270
Let's look at a bad example.

47
00:04:47,025 --> 00:04:50,410
The sizes shouldn't be uniform.

48
00:04:50,411 --> 00:04:54,250
You shouldn't give density everywhere.

49
00:04:54,251 --> 00:04:58,970
Point colors shouldn't pop out in every place.

50
00:04:58,971 --> 00:05:04,810
This is not just for clothing design, but also for illustration composition.

51
00:05:04,811 --> 00:05:10,931
This class is a concept art class, but let me briefly talk about illustration too.

52
00:05:12,355 --> 00:05:20,670
This gumiho illustration naturally concentrated all the density in the center, to direct the gaze to the character.

53
00:05:24,570 --> 00:05:32,821
By creating a flow that spreads out from the center, the gaze can naturally concentrate.

54
00:05:33,640 --> 00:05:40,200
The objects at the outside blend into the background, so they don't steal the gaze much.

55
00:05:43,745 --> 00:05:45,580
This picture is the same too.

56
00:05:45,581 --> 00:05:52,501
It's overall blue-toned, but only the skin is shown in a complement color, concentrating the gaze.

57
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:06,030
The purple of these marbles could also be a point color, but I painted it so it doesn't pop out too much toward the outside.

58
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:13,550
This Widowmaker painting is the same principle.

59
00:06:13,551 --> 00:06:24,510
When I decided to sit this character in this chair, and first considered the color and design, the most important factor was that I mustn't disperse the gaze.

60
00:06:26,010 --> 00:06:31,180
What are the concentrated and released parts of this character?

61
00:06:31,181 --> 00:06:41,950
I didn't design it, but if I analyze it, first would be this skin tone and the purple top tone.

62
00:06:44,675 --> 00:06:50,350
The red lens of the visor here could be a point too.

63
00:06:50,351 --> 00:06:58,711
Anyway, the point elements of the upper body are mostly concentrated, and I also wanted the gaze to go to the face.

64
00:06:59,960 --> 00:07:03,050
The released part is the dark part.

65
00:07:03,130 --> 00:07:08,131
It's the dark clothing parts and the coat part like this.

66
00:07:12,195 --> 00:07:18,510
So I likewise put this dark grayish color on the chair too.

67
00:07:18,511 --> 00:07:31,500
As a result, the gaze concentrates only toward this character's skin here, and I designed it so the gaze can flow naturally like a gradient.

68
00:07:34,070 --> 00:07:42,040
In this process, the red lens of the visor doesn't help the gaze concentrate, so I drew it to have as little presence as possible.

69
00:07:44,670 --> 00:07:53,671
This picture collides various colors and value contrasts.

70
00:07:56,035 --> 00:08:00,670
Reducing contrast toward the outside.

71
00:08:02,760 --> 00:08:07,880
Concentrating the gaze on this side.

72
00:08:07,881 --> 00:08:09,560
And through various designs.

73
00:08:12,245 --> 00:08:16,631
Creating this flow of gaze.

74
00:08:22,805 --> 00:08:27,111
If the belt of this uniform were hanging down, say.

75
00:08:28,469 --> 00:08:30,200
It'd become an element that breaks the flow.

76
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:34,440
It wouldn't be good.

77
00:08:35,685 --> 00:08:38,920
Anyway, in this way, you can create density with light too.

78
00:08:40,190 --> 00:08:46,240
This picture is the design of illustrator Okku, drawn in my own style.

79
00:08:46,241 --> 00:08:57,870
Looking at this picture, it uses the characteristics of backlight, that is, total internal reflection, to draw out the concentration of the gaze.

80
00:08:57,871 --> 00:08:59,630
We learned about total internal reflection.

81
00:09:00,780 --> 00:09:12,270
In this way, density brings about concentration of the gaze, and concentration of the gaze lets you appreciate the picture longer and makes it stay in the viewer's memory for a long time.

82
00:09:18,435 --> 00:09:25,230
We've learned about the importance and method of density, so let's learn how to work it.

83
00:09:25,231 --> 00:09:29,630
I said I first start by simply grasping the masses.

84
00:09:29,631 --> 00:09:33,950
I have a plan for density, but I haven't drawn it yet.

85
00:09:33,951 --> 00:09:37,870
Because I think it's good to create the masses, then carve them out.

86
00:09:39,320 --> 00:09:42,890
You don't hang a picture frame before applying the wallpaper.

87
00:09:42,891 --> 00:09:46,490
You don't apply eyeshadow before base makeup.

88
00:09:46,491 --> 00:09:51,150
After grasping the masses, I draw the details or patterns with lines.

89
00:09:53,500 --> 00:09:56,750
You can also paste photos, but I don't like that.

90
00:09:56,751 --> 00:10:01,390
For the method of pasting photos, take a matte painting lecture.

91
00:10:01,391 --> 00:10:05,150
Anyway, since I've decided to draw, it'll be the start of another boring task.

92
00:10:06,390 --> 00:10:13,240
This is a pattern based on historical accuracy, so it was less difficult, but if you try to create something original, the pain begins.

93
00:10:13,241 --> 00:10:16,481
At this time, we can reference photo materials.

94
00:10:17,530 --> 00:10:22,060
What's really important here is that I told you not to put up references from the start.

95
00:10:23,100 --> 00:10:26,380
You need to have a plan for the pattern to some extent.

96
00:10:26,381 --> 00:10:30,021
That, like, what nuance I'll draw the pattern in.

97
00:10:30,760 --> 00:10:31,760
For example.

98
00:10:33,750 --> 00:10:40,520
If you want to draw a dragon statue like this, first draw it as much as you can within your ability.

99
00:10:40,521 --> 00:10:44,721
After that, look up dragon statues.

100
00:10:45,110 --> 00:10:50,680
It's not like you find some random pattern, think it looks cool, put it up and copy it.

101
00:10:53,709 --> 00:10:58,025
After drawing the sketch lines, lay down the base color broadly like this.

102
00:11:02,550 --> 00:11:03,960
Then you'd render the value.

103
00:11:07,670 --> 00:11:09,560
And do correction too.

104
00:11:12,990 --> 00:11:17,721
Complete it through the process of adding and refining detail.

105
00:11:21,975 --> 00:11:31,840
The time this pattern-insertion work takes is important and difficult, accounting for thirty to fifty percent of the whole process.

106
00:11:31,841 --> 00:11:35,600
It's important to plant your glutes firmly and draw with patience.

107
00:11:38,750 --> 00:11:46,240
Even if you render the released parts less, it's good to render the parts you decided to give density to, clearly and cleanly.

108
00:11:50,680 --> 00:11:54,330
Now let's also talk about finishing a picture.

109
00:11:54,410 --> 00:11:57,731
If you worked hard on a picture but the finish is sloppy, that's not good, right?

110
00:11:58,490 --> 00:12:02,250
Correction helps with finishing.

111
00:12:02,251 --> 00:12:05,331
We'll cover correction in a later chapter.

112
00:12:06,390 --> 00:12:08,040
How do you crop?

113
00:12:08,041 --> 00:12:11,960
In the case of concept art, most pictures are tall, top to bottom.

114
00:12:11,961 --> 00:12:15,240
I tend to cut the top and bottom as much as possible.

115
00:12:15,241 --> 00:12:19,041
So the character looks big when displayed on a monitor.

116
00:12:20,375 --> 00:12:25,961
That said, you shouldn't crop so much that you leave no breathing room.

117
00:12:32,080 --> 00:12:40,880
In illustration, you often focus on the grid lines divided by threes.

118
00:12:45,295 --> 00:12:50,200
An image with transparency in the background, so-called cutout, we call it.

119
00:12:50,201 --> 00:12:53,160
It's a word derived from Japanese.

120
00:12:53,240 --> 00:13:03,720
If you need to use a cutout image like this, you need to cleanly tidy the image's outline and remove brush marks.

121
00:13:03,721 --> 00:13:08,161
Try changing the background to black and a clear primary color family.

122
00:13:11,875 --> 00:13:16,350
It's good to check.

123
00:13:16,351 --> 00:13:19,870
Let's also talk about resizing a picture.

124
00:13:19,871 --> 00:13:29,450
For ease of work, you usually work at a large size.

125
00:13:29,451 --> 00:13:31,530
But resizing is sometimes necessary.

126
00:13:33,850 --> 00:13:45,461
When uploading to the web, being forcibly resized is unavoidable, but when sending your work to someone else, it's good to limit the height so the picture appears on screen without resizing.

127
00:13:46,885 --> 00:13:51,840
Because in a viewer, if it's forcibly resized, the quality breaks.

128
00:13:51,841 --> 00:13:56,000
So I usually resize to match the screen resolution.

129
00:13:56,001 --> 00:14:09,120
When I worked at a company, the staff were issued 2560 by 1440 resolution monitors, so I set the height below 1440.

130
00:14:10,550 --> 00:14:17,160
Then, when I open the file, I can see the picture clearly without resizing.

131
00:14:19,610 --> 00:14:28,620
When saving a picture, you can use various extensions, but commonly used are JPEG and PNG.

132
00:14:28,700 --> 00:14:31,980
PNG has no loss compared to JPEG.

133
00:14:31,981 --> 00:14:39,580
Unless it's a special case, this PNG has a larger file size, but I want to save as close to the original as possible.

134
00:14:39,581 --> 00:14:43,301
If you want to use transparency, you should save as PNG.

135
00:14:44,920 --> 00:14:48,771
If the picture needs to reduce file size for the web.

136
00:14:49,880 --> 00:14:53,450
This JPEG isn't a bad choice either.

137
00:14:54,910 --> 00:15:01,481
And regardless of file size, if you want to save the original without compression, save as BMP.

138
00:15:07,155 --> 00:15:08,460
The explanation got long.

139
00:15:08,510 --> 00:15:11,781
Based on what we've learned, let's begin finishing the picture.

140
00:15:12,755 --> 00:15:18,140
I'll intensively render the face and shoulder areas where I was trying to reduce density.

141
00:15:18,141 --> 00:15:23,100
Simply, you just need to invest as much as half the time you've drawn so far.

142
00:15:23,101 --> 00:15:27,340
I said I spend thirty to fifty percent of the whole process on density rendering.

143
00:15:29,289 --> 00:15:30,730
Of course, it's boring.

144
00:15:30,731 --> 00:15:37,290
But you can think that, for my own worth, this amount of time investment is well worth doing.

145
00:15:37,370 --> 00:15:42,250
I drew this picture about five hours, so wouldn't sketching like this... well, drawing it a couple more hours be fine?

146
00:15:44,145 --> 00:15:46,490
I wanted to do it while watching YouTube, but I held back.

147
00:15:47,425 --> 00:15:50,691
Drawing while filming isn't easy.

148
00:15:51,775 --> 00:15:54,090
Honestly, I don't like the picture.

149
00:15:54,091 --> 00:15:59,210
Normally I'd redraw it, but since I've already filmed this video, I can't help it.

150
00:16:00,540 --> 00:16:07,111
I want to revisit the concept and design of this picture later and redraw it thoroughly.

151
00:19:58,459 --> 00:20:05,221
I accidentally failed to film about twenty minutes of video, but anyway I did the rendering this way.

152
00:20:07,555 --> 00:20:09,790
Rendering is a battle against time.

153
00:20:09,791 --> 00:20:15,790
I've rendered up to this point, but that's not to say there's no more room to draw.

154
00:20:15,791 --> 00:20:19,650
In concept art, honestly, this level is enough.

155
00:20:20,225 --> 00:20:24,750
Because you can explain the detailed design in a design sheet.

156
00:20:24,751 --> 00:20:32,110
When you work at a company or do this kind of commercial work, there come times when you have to stop detailed rendering at the right moment.

157
00:20:32,111 --> 00:20:36,430
Even if my artistic mindset rises up and makes me want to render even harder.

158
00:20:36,919 --> 00:20:40,090
In a work context, the schedule is more important.

159
00:20:40,091 --> 00:20:42,410
You need to allocate time well and work.

160
00:20:44,919 --> 00:20:48,970
This time, we learned about the meaning of detail.

161
00:20:48,970 --> 00:20:53,210
Next time, let's learn about various correction methods for a picture.
